── (2) Genuinely activatable, not just visually tappable ───────────────────
Mechanical/structural proof (airtight given (1)): with EXACTLY ONE node per
row and that node's OWN `clickable="true"` (i.e. Flutter's engine set
`AccessibilityNodeInfo.addAction(ACTION_CLICK)` / `isClickable()` on THIS
node, not a sibling), any assistive-technology action targeting this screen
location can only resolve to this one node's `ACTION_CLICK` — there is no
second, wrongly-activatable node left to land on (which was exactly cycle-2's
failure shape: a same-bounds non-checkable View absorbing the tap while the
real control sat elsewhere). Source (`n_settings_tile.dart` buildContent()):
the outer `Semantics(container:true, explicitChildNodes:true, button:
tappable, enabled: ..., onTap: enabled ? onTap : null, toggled:
_trailingSwitchValue, child: ExcludeSemantics(child: row))` re-supplies
`onTap` on itself exactly per this repo's own active review lesson
`dls-semantics-missing-container-boundary` ("the wrapper MUST re-supply
onTap: itself" whenever excludeSemantics/explicitChildNodes isolates a
child subtree) — this is the documented failure mode the lesson exists to
catch, and the code takes the prescribed corrective shape.

Live/behavioral proof (end-to-end, not just structural): tapped each row's
live bounds and observed the real callback fire both times:
  - Offers row tap -> `[NET] PUT endpoint=/api/v1/customer/notification-
    preferences` -> `200`, switch flips (see (3)).
  - Order-updates row tap (row was ON, so onTap goes through the confirm-gate
    branch, not an immediate flip) -> opened the
    "Turn off order updates?" NotificationStatusChangeBottomSheet
    (`📊 analytics: screen_view {screen: notification_confirm_sheet}`),
    confirming with "Yes" then flips the switch (see (3)).
Both rows' `onTap` therefore resolves to the correct, distinct handler
(`_handleOrdersRowTap` vs `_handleOffersRowTap`), not a shared/wrong one.

Residual tooling limitation (carried over from cycle-1, not new to this
cycle, not a product defect): this pool's `adb shell input tap` does not
reliably route through TalkBack's touch-exploration layer (same root cause
already documented for `input swipe` in userapp-navigation-guide.md's
NEARS-2901 gotcha — synthetic 2-point motion injection lacks the
intermediate samples Android's gesture detector needs), so a live tap here
cannot by itself distinguish "activated via a real double-tap-to-activate
AT gesture" from "activated via the app's own raw touch/InkWell hit test".
This toolset gap is why sub-claim (2)'s decisive evidence is the structural
argument above (single node + that node's own clickable flag + re-supplied
onTap per the DLS's own documented pattern) rather than a captured TalkBack
double-tap trace. Flagged plainly, not glossed over — same limitation cycle-1
already hit and documented; not attempted further within this cycle's scope
per the existing tooling-gap note.

── (3) toggled/checked correctly reflects real state after toggling ────────
Offers & promotions: true -> tap -> re-dump shows checked="false"
(NEARS-3701-after-offers-toggle-cycle3.xml), single node, content-desc
unchanged, clickable="true" retained. Tapped again -> back to checked="true"
(restored to original state).

Order updates: true -> tap -> confirm sheet -> "Yes" -> re-dump shows
checked="false" (NEARS-3701-after-orders-toggle-cycle3.xml), single node.
Tapped again (ON is immediate, no confirm gate) -> back to checked="true"
(restored to original state); confirmed via
`📊 analytics: notification_pref_changed {channel: orders, enabled: 1, ...}`
firing on the restore tap.

